# Obvious Code (Grug-Style)

**Complexity is the enemy.** Write code a junior developer can read and change without guessing.

## Do

- **Plain names**: `getUserById`, `saveToDatabase`, `formatDate`—what it does, not clever puns
- **Straight-line logic**: Prefer `if/else` and early returns over deeply nested logic
- **One job per function**: Small functions with clear inputs/outputs
- **Explicit over clever**: Extra lines are fine if they make the flow obvious
- **Comment the why, not the what**: Code shows *what*; comments explain *why* when it’s not obvious

## Don’t

- **Abstract too early**: Wait until you see the same pattern 2–3 times; avoid premature factories, base classes, layers
- **Chain abstractions**: Avoid `AbstractBaseFactory.create()`—junior must trace too many files
- **Use “smart” idioms**: Ternary chains, nested `.reduce`, clever one-liners—readability before brevity
- **Hide complexity**: Put tricky logic in a tiny helper with a vague name so the caller looks simple

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ Complexity demon—what does this do?
const r = items.reduce((a, i) => ({ ...a, [i.k]: i.v }), {});

// ✅ Obvious—junior gets it
const result: Record<string, string> = {};
for (const item of items) {
  result[item.key] = item.value;
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ Too clever
const out = x ? y ? z : a : b;

// ✅ Readable
let out;
if (x) {
  out = y ? z : a;
} else {
  out = b;
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ Premature abstraction—junior must jump 3 files to understand
const handler = createEventHandler(config, middleware);

// ✅ Inline and obvious (until you truly need to reuse)
function handleClick(event: MouseEvent) {
  const target = event.target as HTMLElement;
  if (!target.closest('.active')) return;
  doTheThing(target);
}
```

## Grug reminder

*Complexity demon enters through “helpful” abstractions and clever code. Keep it simple. Junior should understand at first read.*
